      "dom_id": "question-101316",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1}=$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both the numerator and the denominator by the highest power of $x$ in the denominator, which is ${x}^{3}.$ Then, by noting that the reciprocals shrink to $0$ in the limit, we can then evaluate the resulting expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{6}+7{x}^{3}-9}{2{x}^{3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{7{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(2{x}^{3}+7-\\frac{9}{{x}^{3}})}{(2-\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2{x}^{3}+7-0}{2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}+7) \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Incorrect",
